  <p>
    PL-9643 takes the melanocortin system somewhere unexpected: the eye. It began as one of
    Palatin Technologies' investigational melanocortin agonists — this one an eye drop for
    <strong>dry eye disease</strong>, and like <a href="/pl-8177">PL-8177</a> in the gut,
    it is built on the system's anti-inflammatory arm rather than its pigment or appetite
    roles.<Cite n={1} /> Unlike PL-8177, which is tuned to MC1R alone, PL-9643 is a
    <strong>pan-agonist</strong>: it activates every melanocortin receptor except the
    adrenal <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a>, on the reasoning that the eye's
    anti-inflammatory biology runs through more than one of them.<Cite n={1} /><Cite n={7} />
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="Signs and symptoms. Dry-eye trials are judged on two kinds of endpoint at once — what the patient feels and what the clinician measures. MELODY-1 cleared the first column and not the second, which is why two further trials must show both."
    source="Schematic, after Palatin's MELODY-1 topline (2024) and ARVO 2025 analyses."
  >
    <SignsAndSymptoms />
  </Figure>

  <h2>Calming the surface, not just wetting it</h2>
  <p>
    Dry eye disease is not only a lubrication problem; a large part of it is
    <strong>inflammation</strong> of the ocular surface that becomes self-sustaining.
    PL-9643 activates melanocortin receptors on the eye to dampen that inflammation and
    push it toward resolution — the same broad biology that makes{' '}
    <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a> a natural anti-inflammatory signal, applied
    topically. That mechanism is different both from artificial tears, which lubricate,
    and from broad immunosuppressants, which blunt immunity generally. The{' '}
    <a href="/inflammation">inflammation</a> page explains why melanocortin agonists do
    not merely switch inflammation off but help tissue return to baseline.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the eye is a natural home for this biology</h2>
  <p>
    The eye is one of the body's immune-privileged sites, and α-MSH is part of how it
    stays that way: the peptide is present in the fluid inside the eye, where it helps
    keep immune cells from mounting the kind of response that would damage the retina. In
    a mouse model of autoimmune uveitis, α-MSH and a melanocortin pan-agonist suppressed
    the disease and preserved the retina, an MC1R-only agonist did so only temporarily,
    and agonists lacking MC5R activity did not — which is why the receptor that matters
    for ocular inflammation appears to be MC1R and MC5R together, and why a pan-agonist
    rather than a selective one was chosen for the eye.<Cite n={7} /> On the ocular
    surface itself, α-MSH protects corneal epithelial cells in a rat model of dry eye and
    in human cells under the hyperosmolar stress that characterises the
    disease,<Cite n={8} /> and in people with dry eye the levels of α-MSH and other
    neuropeptides in tears are altered and track with the severity of their
    symptoms.<Cite n={9} /> The pieces were in place before the drug was.
  </p>

  <h2>The Phase 2 lesson</h2>
  <p>
    PL-9643's Phase 2 trial is worth knowing about because it shaped everything after
    it. In a 12-week, placebo-controlled study of 160 patients dosed three times a day,
    the drop did not beat vehicle on its two primary endpoints — corneal staining and
    ocular discomfort — in the overall population. But in the 53 patients with
    moderate-to-severe disease it improved several objective signs, including corneal
    and conjunctival staining and tear break-up time, and it produced no drug-related
    adverse events or discontinuations.<Cite n={1} /> That is the pattern of a drug
    whose effect is real but small relative to the noise of a mild population, and the
    Phase 3 programme was designed around it: moderate-to-severe patients, a vehicle
    run-in to settle the placebo response, and endpoints chosen from the subgroup
    signals.
  </p>

  <h2>The Phase 3 result</h2>
  <p>
    PL-9643's pivotal trial, <strong>MELODY-1</strong>, enrolled 575 patients with
    moderate-to-severe dry eye at US sites, with a four-week run-in followed by twelve
    weeks of treatment. It met its co-primary <em>symptom</em> endpoint of ocular pain
    (P&lt;0.025) along with several secondary symptom endpoints, with symptom relief
    emerging by week 2 and continuing to build through 12 weeks — and it did so with
    <em>fewer</em> ocular adverse events than the vehicle control (5.6% against 6.3%)
    and fewer discontinuations.<Cite n={2} /> It did <em>not</em>, however, meet the
    other co-primary endpoint — a <em>sign</em> of disease (conjunctival lissamine green
    staining), the objective, physician-graded measure that dry-eye drugs have long found
    hardest to move; secondary sign endpoints trended in the right direction without
    reaching significance.<Cite n={2} /> Updated analyses presented in 2025 found six of
    thirteen symptom endpoints statistically significant, and a responder analysis in
    which a share of patients reached complete resolution of symptoms, a result the
    company has stressed no approved dry-eye therapy has shown.<Cite n={3} />
    Independent coverage framed it as a promising, potentially first-in-class
    approach.<Cite n={5} />
  </p>
  <p>
    Why signs are hard matters for reading the result. Dry-eye regulators in the United
    States have historically wanted a drug to improve both what the patient feels and
    what the clinician measures, and the two often move independently: staining scores
    are variable, fluctuate with the weather and the examiner, and have sunk many drugs
    that patients said helped. The FDA has since confirmed that the design and endpoints
    of the remaining two trials, <strong>MELODY-2</strong> and <strong>MELODY-3</strong>,
    are acceptable; each is built to evaluate both the signs and the symptoms of the
    disease — the fuller bar a dry-eye drug must clear.<Cite n={4} />
  </p>

  <h2>Where the programme stands</h2>
  <p>
    The programme has changed hands. Palatin, refocusing on its melanocortin obesity
    assets, sublicensed PL-9643 for dry eye to Altanispac Labs in early 2026, which is
    now responsible for the remaining Phase 3 trials; their start depends on
    funding.<Cite n={6} /> The <a href="/pipeline">pipeline</a> page tracks it as a
    Phase 3 asset, which is accurate for the stage reached, with the honest caveat that
    the next trial has not yet begun.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    PL-9643 is investigational, and a single positive symptom trial is a milestone, not an
    approval — the remaining MELODY studies still have to deliver, and on signs as well as
    symptoms. But it is a striking demonstration of the melanocortin system's reach: the
    same anti-inflammatory signalling behind pigment biology and gut disease, formulated
    as an eye drop and aimed at a tissue the system already helps keep quiet. For the
    underlying biology, see <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/inflammation">the anti-inflammatory arm</a>; for the wider landscape, see
    the <a href="/pipeline">melanocortin pipeline</a>.
  </p>

